    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "Re:Build, which spun out Slate Auto, raises $2.1B",
      "d": "The manufacturing accelerator bills itself as \"America’s answer to Shenzhen.\"",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/27/rebuild-which-spun-out-slate-auto-raises-2-1b/",
      "m": "10. 27. PM 08:31",
      "ts": 1793133077000,
      "fullText": "Re:Build, the Massachusetts-based manufacturing accelerator that spun out EV startup Slate Auto in 2023, has raised $2.1 billion The company said Tuesday that it will use the funding to &#8220;expand Re:Build’s capacity to manufacture hard tech products and launch new industrial startups,&#8221; which will &#8220;ensure the most innovative products can be made in America for generations to come.&#8221; The investment was led by General Catalyst and JPMorganChase&#8217;s Strategic Investment Group. Other investors weren&#8217;t disclosed. The funding round comes as increasing attention and capital is flowing to the idea of building up manufacturing and supply chains in the United States, especially as a hedge away from China. &#8220;We started Re:Build because the deindustrialization of America is an economic failure and national security risk,” Re:Build co-founder Jeff Wilke said in a statement. &#8220;This capital allows Re:Build to go faster and further than would otherwise be possible, and build a great manufacturing business and a platform for American industrial leadership. The products and companies emerging from our ecosystem prove we can help restore America’s industrial might.&#8221; General Catalyst&#8217;s CEO Hemant Taneja echoed those sentiments in a statement, saying, &#8220;General Catalyst has long believed that rebuilding American industrial capacity is one of the most important economic and national security imperatives of our time.&#8221; Taneja added: &#8220;To compete with China, re-industrialize the U.S., and revitalize the towns and jobs that have been left behind, we need platform companies that can operate at genuine scale — and we need our innovation ecosystem to learn how to build hardware again.&#8221; Founded in 2020, Re:Build bills itself as &#8220;America’s answer to Shenzhen,&#8221; and it has numerous ties to Amazon. It was co-founded by Wilke, who was Amazon&#8217;s former commercial CEO. Wei Gao, a longtime Amazon VP and technical "
    },
